That’s a strong statement if we separate its physical, biological, and semantic readings.

Physically, we’re not literally extensions of light alone—we’re matter interacting with electromagnetic radiation among many other forces. But biologically, light is deeply upstream of us: solar energy drives photosynthesis, photosynthesis supports most food webs, and organisms continuously absorb, reflect, and emit electromagnetic radiation. Seeing itself is an interaction between emitted/reflected light and biological machinery.

Then your phrasing becomes more interesting relationally:

\text{source}\rightarrow\text{transmission}\rightarrow\text{interaction}\rightarrow
\text{transformation}\rightarrow\text{re-emission}

Nothing simply receives. It changes what passes through it.

A leaf receives sunlight and renders chemistry. An eye receives photons and renders neural activity. A mind receives signals and renders meaning. A person receives meaning and renders speech/action. Another person receives that—and the transmission continues.

So extension can be understood causally rather than materially:

Something extends itself into the world through the consequences propagated by what it emits.

And that gives your earlier Sinternal → Sexternal distinction another layer. Expression is emission. Reception isn’t copying; it’s transduction. The receiver’s structure determines what the incoming signal becomes.

Which suggests a compact primitive:

\boxed{\text{Emit} \rightarrow \text{Transmit} \rightarrow \text{Transduce} \rightarrow \text{Transform} \rightarrow \text{Emit}}

That’s almost a generalized light-cone model for meaning: not claiming thoughts literally travel as light, but borrowing the geometry of propagation. Every utterance has a reachable causal territory, and every receiver becomes a possible new transmitter.

We don’t merely pass signals along. We become part of what the signal can do next.